{"pagination":{"maxPageableSet":"10000","numFound":3,"query":"name=Didion&limit=10&title=The Year of Magical Thinking","limit":10,"start":0},"items":{"mods":[{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"nonSort":{"@space":"preserve","#text":"The  "},"title":"year of magical thinking "},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Didion, Joan","role":[{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}},{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"author"}}]},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"biography"},{"@authority":"rdacontent","#text":"text"},{"@authority":"Library of Congress Subject Headings","#text":"Autobiography."},{"@authority":"Medical Subject Headings","#text":"Biography"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Autobiographies"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Biographies"},{"@authority":"lcgft","#text":"Biographies"},{"@authority":"rbgenr","#text":"Autobiographies."},{"@authority":"rvmgf","#text":"Biographies."}],"originInfo":[{"place":{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nyu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New York (State)"}]},"dateIssued":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2005"},"issuance":"monographic","edition":"First edition."},{"@eventType":"publication","place":{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York :"}},"agent":{"namePart":"Alfred A. Knopf,","role":{"roleTerm":"publisher"}},"dateIssued":"2005."}],"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":[{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},{"@authority":"marccategory","#text":"text"},{"@authority":"marcsmd","#text":"regular print"},{"@type":"media","@authority":"rdamedia","#text":"unmediated"},{"@type":"carrier","@authority":"rdacarrier","#text":"volume"}],"extent":"227 pages ; 21 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"[In this book, the author] explores an intensely personal yet universal experience: a portrait of a marriage - and a life, in good times and bad - that will speak to anyone who has ever loved a husband or wife or child. Several days before Christmas 2003, John Gregory Dunne and Joan Didion saw their only daughter, Quintana, fall ill with what seemed at first flu, then pneumonia, then complete septic shock. She was put into an induced coma and placed on life support. Days later - the night before New Year's Eve - the Dunnes were just sitting down to dinner after visiting the hospital when John Gregory Dunne suffered a massive and fatal coronary. In a second, this close, symbiotic partnership of forty years was over. Four weeks later, their daughter pulled through. Two months after that, arriving at LAX, she collapsed and underwent six hours of brain surgery at UCLA Medical Center to relieve a massive hematoma. This ... book is Didion's attempt to make sense of the \"weeks and then months that cut loose any fixed idea I ever had about death, about illness ... about marriage and children and memory ... about the shallowness of sanity, about life itself.\"--Jacket."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Joan Didion."},"National Book Award, Nonfiction, 2005","BookBrowse Ruby Award for Most Popular 2005 Book by Category Winner, 2006"],"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"n-us---"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Novelists, American","Family relationships"],"temporal":"20th century"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Novelists, American","temporal":"20th century","genre":"Biography"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Journalists","geographic":"United States","genre":"Biography"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Mothers and daughters","geographic":"United States"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Widows","geographic":"United States","genre":"Biography"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Loss (Psychology)"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Grief"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Mothers"},{"@authority":"lcshac","topic":"Mothers and daughters"